WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Critical Connections Between Measurements and Statistics

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.433
Welcome back to this session on the power of measurement.

3
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:12.299
Now, recall before we took a look at a simple line

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:19.266
and found that that line was three and a quarter inches long from point A

5
00:00:19.266 --> 00:00:23.999
to point B. We asked, if we didn't have measurements how would we characterize it?

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.166
Some might say well it's a straight line. Well, my question would become how straight?

7
00:00:30.166 --> 00:00:37.132
Nothing is perfectly straight. How big is the gap? Some might say well it's a long line.

8
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:42.933
And I say, well what constitutes long?

9
00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:49.966
That changes greatly from person to person, on their idea of what's long and what’s short.

10
00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:54.632
And what’s a medium line?

11
00:00:54.633 --> 00:00:58.866
Well you see, the more that we can move away from this kind of subjectivity

12
00:00:58.866 --> 00:01:02.666
to repeatable verifiable fact. Which is measurement.

13
00:01:02.666 --> 00:01:06.032
In its purest form, is measurement.

14
00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:09.366
The more we can all carry around the same kind of knowledge.

15
00:01:09.366 --> 00:01:12.366
Now, let's take a look at this number.

16
00:01:12.366 --> 00:01:19.332
As a matter of fact let’s truncate the quarter-inch and just look at the number three.

17
00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:26.299
What is this thing, three? That's an interesting, interesting concept.

18
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:33.100
A number. Matter of fact I’m going to highlight it. What does that number three mean?

19
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:39.200
Well it's merely a hieroglyph. That's an interesting word hieroglyph.

20
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:45.566
Another word for that is symbol. It’s just a symbol. It stands

21
00:01:45.566 --> 00:01:51.399
for three independent ones. See, that's a one. That's a single occurrence.

22
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:58.000
So things in this world, are either present, one, or their absent, zero.

23
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.500
So if I have three things that are present,

24
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:09.600
then I would say there are one, two, three.

25
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:16.600
Equals the symbol three. This is an operation that I perform on that.

26
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:23.566
1, 1, 1 is three. Now, if I added one more 1 to that.

27
00:02:23.566 --> 00:02:29.399
The symbol would now be 4. So again, it’s just a symbol,

28
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:36.366
that constitutes for a slot. Now, let me explain what I mean by that. Let's suppose,

29
00:02:36.366 --> 00:02:43.066
that we have

30
00:02:43.066 --> 00:02:50.032
four opportunities. Slots if you will. In which some occurrence

31
00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:53.999
would occur. Either there’s something in that slot, or there isn't.

32
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.833
Therefore, the slots can assume one of two conditions,

33
00:02:58.833 --> 00:03:03.833
zero or one, zero or one, zero or one, zero or one.

34
00:03:03.833 --> 00:03:10.766
Now let's suppose for the sake of argument, on the first time through this slot was zero.

35
00:03:10.766 --> 00:03:14.666
This one was one, zero, one.

36
00:03:14.666 --> 00:03:21.699
Then numerically, we recognize that all that added together is equal to

37
00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:26.166
two. Two of the four slots or

38
00:03:26.166 --> 00:03:32.666
50% of the slots had an occurrence. See we’re measuring at this point.

39
00:03:32.666 --> 00:03:38.032
We’re measuring, we’re measuring on the most elemental level, that there is.

40
00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:44.599
And that number two, is simply symbolic of the presence of something,

41
00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:51.000
as contrasted to the non-presence of it. So again, it’s just a symbol.

42
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:58.000
That number two, or the number three, or the number four,

43
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.400
or the number five, six, seven, and on and on and on, doesn't care.

44
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:10.266
It doesn't care where it come from. It doesn't care whether it's the simple

45
00:04:10.266 --> 00:04:13.232
measurement of a line.

46
00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:18.266
Whether it's the radio frequency of an electrical device.

47
00:04:18.266 --> 00:04:25.066
Whether it's the speed of an automobile, or whether it's the service

48
00:04:25.066 --> 00:04:30.166
provided to you by a landscaper. Now, it doesn't really care.

49
00:04:30.166 --> 00:04:35.466
That number two could have been the number of times, he or she mowed your yard.

50
00:04:35.466 --> 00:04:39.999
It could be the number of flowers that they picked from your garden.

51
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.933
It could be the miles per gallon in your automobile.

52
00:04:42.933 --> 00:04:46.333
It could be the number of miles that your radio transmits.

53
00:04:46.333 --> 00:04:49.399
It could be the length of a line.

54
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.900
That number once you get it. It doesn't really care.

55
00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:57.000
And you see, when we do statistics on those numbers.

56
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.000
We just simply have a collection of numbers. Here's some numbers.

57
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.733
The number two, the number three, the number four, and so on. That's what these X’s are.

58
00:05:05.733 --> 00:05:10.533
These are just numbers of things. They don't really care. Statistics don’t care.

59
00:05:10.533 --> 00:05:14.133
It just simply goes in here and crunches that numbers.

60
00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:21.066
Cause we got to remember something. Got to remember a word called torture.

61
00:05:21.066 --> 00:05:25.832
Data in raw form is not information. It's just numbers.

62
00:05:25.833 --> 00:05:32.433
You apply statistics to numbers, to torture it,

63
00:05:32.433 --> 00:05:38.033
to get it to confess what it knows. See statistics is like a torture tool.

64
00:05:38.033 --> 00:05:43.733
It just tortures data until it confesses what it knows.

65
00:05:43.733 --> 00:05:50.733
So, statistics is a way to take those abstract numbers and synthesize them

66
00:05:50.733 --> 00:05:56.099
down to tables, charts,

67
00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:59.766
and summary numbers.

68
00:05:59.766 --> 00:06:05.899
That helps to describe some object, situation, or phenomenon.

69
00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:12.666
That's really what the numbers do and those numbers come from standard scales,

70
00:06:12.666 --> 00:06:18.166
like a ruler. That we put on things to judge

71
00:06:18.166 --> 00:06:24.032
extent, volume,

72
00:06:24.033 --> 00:06:30.133
magnitude, etc. The magnitude is something. The volume is something.

73
00:06:30.133 --> 00:06:35.799
Or the extent to which something occurs. That's what we use these numbers for.

74
00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:41.733
Is to describe that. And then we statistics to operate on those numbers, to torture it

75
00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:46.266
to provide us the tables that tells us ultimately what we want to know.

76
00:06:46.266 --> 00:06:49.332
That's really the power of measurement.

77
00:06:49.333 --> 00:06:54.333
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
